# Fixtures are compared byte for byte, so git must not rewrite them.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default) a checkout turns an LF blob
# into CRLF on disk. For source that is harmless -- rustc and rustfmt do not
# care. For a fixture it is not, and it fails in the worst possible way: the
# suite is green in the working tree and red in a fresh clone, because the
# working tree is not a faithful copy of the repository.
#
# Two independent instances of that, both found by cloning clean and running
# the suite there:
#
#   crates/rar-codec/testdata/rar/rar5-body.expected
#   crates/rgx-adapters-archive/testdata/rar/rar4-lz29.expected
#   crates/rgx-adapters-archive/testdata/rar/rar5-body.expected
#
# were stored as LF but hold bytes that came out of a RAR archive, where they
# are CRLF. The checkout rewrote the LF blobs, which by accident fixed the
# three body fixtures and broke rar5-note.expected (whose payload really is
# LF) in the same stroke. Two opposite errors cancelling out on one machine.
#
# Five PDF fixtures were outright corrupted the same way, which made the PDF
# adapter bail on its font tests. Git decides text-vs-binary by inspecting the
# blob, and it happened to classify two of the seven PDFs correctly and five
# of them as text -- so any rule has to name them, not ask git.
# Text, LF in the index and on disk. Stated explicitly so the tree does not
# depend on each developer's autocrlf setting -- which also keeps the shell
# scripts runnable on a unix checkout, where a CRLF pgo.sh does not run at
# all.
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.tsv text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
# These two name the rules above, so they are read under the same ones:
# a checkout that rewrote them would rewrite the rules themselves.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
Cargo.lock text eol=lf
# The licence files are text like the rest. The audit reads them, and a
# checkout that rewrote them would leave the tree depending on the
# reader's autocrlf -- one of them came out of a checkout with the
# endings mixed.
COPYING text eol=lf
NOTICE text eol=lf
LICENSE* text eol=lf
# The workflow files carry shell inside YAML: a `run: |` block is a shell
# script, and a CRLF one has a carriage return at the end of every command.
# On a unix runner that is "command not found" for the first line.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.rb text eol=lf
# Nix reads the flake's bytes and hashes them (the narHash in flake.lock and
# the source tree hash of a path: flake). A CRLF checkout is a different
# flake, so the Nix files are LF in the index and on every disk. flake.lock
# has no extension, so name it too.
*.nix text eol=lf
flake.lock text eol=lf

# The fixtures and the patches, byte for byte -- and THIS is where the rules
# have to be.
#
# In gitattributes the LAST matching pattern wins, so a `-text` rule that
# sits above a `text eol=lf` rule loses, and the fixture is rewritten
# anyway. The order here is the rule, and it was the other way round until
# the drift gate caught it: four testdata READMEs that upstream holds as
# CRLF were committed as LF, because the `*.md text eol=lf` rule sat below
# the `-text` rule and won. The working tree hid it -- `core.autocrlf`
# turned the LF blobs back into CRLF on checkout, so the tree matched
# upstream and the repository did not. A fresh clone showed the difference
# the working tree had hidden, which is the whole failure the rules above
# are written against.
**/testdata/** -text
**/fixtures/** -text
*.expected -text
*.pdf -text
# The README's screenshot is a PNG. Git would sniff it as binary on its own
# -- the signature carries a NUL -- but a rule that is not written is a rule
# that depends on the guess, and the guess is not what keeps a picture a
# picture.
*.png -text
# The clips are MP4, and the README links them: the same rule, for the same
# reason. A checkout that rewrote a clip would put a different movie in the
# release.
*.mp4 -text
# The local patches, byte for byte. `git apply` matches the context lines of
# a patch against the file it is applied to, and a checkout that rewrote the
# line endings would leave every context line ending in a CR that the file
# does not have: the patch would apply to nothing. The patches are LF and
# have to stay LF.
scripts/rgx-local-patches/** -text
# The EGM contract index is generated with explicit LF and is compared byte
# for byte across hosts. No extension means no *.md rule catches it, so a
# Windows checkout with core.autocrlf would otherwise turn it into CRLF and
# make a fresh --reindex disagree with the tracked file on that machine.
.egm/CONTRACTS.index text eol=lf
